#open-question(title: [Macrophagic Myofasciitis Deltoid Biopsy: A Contested Histological Biomarker])[
*(Certainty: n/a — the biopsy is a validated MMF diagnostic, but its status as an ME/CFS biomarker is unresolved.)* Macrophagic myofasciitis (MMF) is a histopathological entity confirmed by deltoid muscle biopsy, showing aluminium-adjuvant particle biopersistence in macrophages. In the single Henri-Mondor referral group, MMF-diagnosed patients present with arthromyalgias, chronic fatigue, and cognitive impairment described as corresponding to ME/CFS @Gherardi2019myalgia @Rigolet2014clinical @VanDerGucht2015spect @VanDerGucht2017fdg @AounSebaiti2018cogn. The open question is whether the deltoid-biopsy MMF finding is a genuine ME/CFS-relevant biomarker or a referral-selection artifact: no study applies formal diagnostic criteria (Fukuda/CCC/ICC/IOM) to show that MMF patients meet ME/CFS, the entire literature derives from one cohort (not independent replication), and the strongest independent population evidence is negative (adjuvanted vaccine HR 0.97 for CFS/ME @Magnus2015influenza).

*Severity applicability:* Unknown — the MMF cohort was not stratified by severity.

*Falsifiable resolution:* An independent, criteria-based (IOM 2015) multicenter study of deltoid-biopsy-confirmed MMF patients would settle whether the biopsy identifies an ME/CFS-enriched subgroup or a referral artifact. Falsified as an ME/CFS biomarker if criteria-based MMF cohorts show no excess of ME/CFS relative to matched controls — the expectation given @Magnus2015influenza.

*Consequence:* Until such independent data exist, a deltoid biopsy positive for MMF should not be used to diagnose ME/CFS or to select a treatment; its value is currently as a documented histopathological finding in a small, referral-biased subgroup, not as a validated ME/CFS biomarker.
] <oq:mmf-deltoid-biopsy-significance>
